### Tin-lined and related perfumer's vessels
<a id="term-tin-lined-vessels"></a>

Meaning: The tin-lined vessel passages show the perfumer's vessel as a managed process tool: stopped, covered, bound, stirred, and later heated indirectly.

Why it matters: This is the clearest recipe evidence that ancient perfume work used a repeatable vessel workflow rather than a single anonymous pot.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-tin-lined-vessels)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-tin-lined-vessels)
Citations: 9

Safe claims:
- Some perfume recipes specify tin or tin-lined perfumer's vessels.
- The same recipe sequences also involve stoppering, covering, binding, soaking, stirring, and later indirect heating.
- The vessel is part of a process chain, not just passive storage.

Do not infer:
- Do not infer that all perfumer's vessels were tin or tin-lined.
- Do not collapse material, craft label, and double construction into one single feature.
- Do not infer one universal vessel shape from these recipes.

### διπλοῦν ἀγγεῖον μυρεψικόν
<a id="term-diployn-aggeion-myrepsikon"></a>

Meaning: Διπλοῦν ἀγγεῖον μυρεψικόν is the explicit phrase for the doubled perfumer's vessel in which materials are soaked or warmed indirectly.

Why it matters: This term gives WP7 the strongest language for the apparatus itself, not just the operation performed in it.

Reader: [full context in tools-texts.md](./tools-texts.md#term-diployn-aggeion-myrepsikon)
Index: [lookup entry](./tools-index.md#term-diployn-aggeion-myrepsikon)
Citations: 4

Safe claims:
- Denotes a doubled perfumer's vessel.
- A smaller vessel can be set into a larger heated one.
- Used for soaking and indirect heating in technical perfume or medical recipes.

Do not infer:
- Do not infer the exact material or exterior shape from the phrase alone.
- Do not assume every doubled vessel was tin-lined.

